  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-THA227</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased decent and productive employment as a result of effective employment services and improved promotion of vocational skills with a focus on the employability of youth and older persons</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Thailand faces demographic and environmental challenges impacting its labour force. A rapidly aging population and rising youth education level contribute to a declining labour force participate rate. Many youths face unemployment and skills mismatch. Environmental concern also calls for the creation of green jobs.  To address this, Thailand&#x2019;s Decent Work Country Programme 2023-27 prioritizes enhancing employment services and lifelong learning. The ILO supports increased decent and productive employment through stronger employment services, inclusive skills and lifelong learning systems, and effective labour market programmes. With a focus on improving the employability of youth and older persons, the programme strengthens national capacity to promote sustainable enterprises, enhance productivity and competitiveness, and expand access to business development and financial services&#x2014;advancing full and productive employment as a pathway to social justice. 
For this biennium, the aim is to build a more inclusive, skilled and sustainable workforce across all stages of live in Thailand:
1. strengthen decent work and responsible business in the automotive sector by capacity building and promoting regular due diligence in supply chains;
2. support disadvantaged youth with technical and employable skills, career guidance, and improve school-to-work transitions;
3. possible business conducts and practices in building resilient and inclusive value chains aligned with sustainable development goals; and
4. advancing skills recognition across ASEAN.</narrative>
  </description>
